# Reelier — record once, replay forever

Reelier (via the `reelier` MCP server, `reelier serve`) can freeze a
deterministic sequence of tool calls into a replayable `SKILL.md`, then
replay it later for zero LLM calls instead of re-reasoning through it.

**Honesty boundary**: Reelier only replays deterministic API/MCP tool
calls — never file edits, shell commands, or other non-deterministic
agent actions. Do not offer to "replay" a coding/editing session; there is
nothing in it Reelier can replay, and it will honestly report an empty
result rather than fabricate a skill.

**When to offer freezing a task**: after finishing a task that was mostly
a repeatable sequence of API/MCP tool calls (data pulls, status checks,
CRUD sequences, ops runbooks) — call `reelier_from_session` on the current
session transcript and report the real result (skill path + stats, or an
honest "nothing replayable" message). Read out any "Open questions" the
compiler flagged before calling the skill trustworthy.

**When to replay instead of redoing**: before manually re-executing a
tool-call sequence you or the user has done before, check for an existing
`*.skill.md` covering it and call `reelier_replay` instead. Report the
real run record either way — never assume it passed.

**Setup** (add to your MCP config, e.g. `~/.cursor/mcp.json`):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "reelier": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "reelier", "serve"]
    }
  }
}
```

Note: `reelier serve` (this tool-server) is a different command from
`reelier mcp` (a recorder that fronts other MCP servers) — don't confuse
the two.
